FIT_MORE_18 += [
('incline-walking-explained', 'Train', 'Incline walking, explained: what the treadmill grade actually does', 'Why a 12% grade at 3mph is a different exercise from a flat walk — the physiology, the settings and the honest expectations.', '<p>Incline walking is the quietest upgrade in fitness: the same movement everyone can already do, with one dial turned up. The treadmill grade setting changes the exercise more than the speed does, and the reasons are worth understanding before the next walk.</p>\n<h2>What the grade number means</h2>\n<p>Treadmill grade is the incline percentage: 1% roughly mimics outdoor terrain resistance at walking speeds; 8-12% is a genuine hill; 15% (the ceiling on most machines) is a steep ramp. The biomechanics shift accordingly &mdash; higher grades raise hip and ankle demands, shift effort into the calves and glutes, and slow the comfortable pace. The physiological picture of graded walking is part of the general exercise-adaptation literature summarised at <a href="https://www.acsm.org/education-resources/trending-topics-resources">ACSM&rsquo;s public resources</a>; the practical reading is simpler: grade buys intensity without the joint cost of speed.</p>\n<h2>Why it works as training</h2>\n<p>Three effects stack up. Heart rate at a given pace rises steeply with grade, so a walk at 3mph and 10% can be genuinely hard aerobic work while staying low-impact. Muscular demand rises too &mdash; the incline walk is a strength-endurance exercise for the posterior chain in a way flat walking is not. And the movement is approachable: no skill, no equipment beyond a ramp, and a natural progression (add grade, then add speed) that beginners can run for months. For the aerobic base it builds, see <a href="/fitness/zone-2-cardio-explained/">zone 2 cardio explained</a>.</p>\n<h2>Settings that matter</h2>\n<p>Hold the handrail question honestly first: gripping the rails cuts the effort and changes the posture &mdash; if balance requires it, lower the grade instead. A durable starting block is 25-40 minutes at 6-10% with a pace you can sustain without bracing; progress by grade first, speed second. Keep a flat warm-up and cool-down of five minutes either side. And if the knees complain, the two dials to turn are grade down and duration up &mdash; the adaptation signal comes from the total work, not the steepest number on the console.</p>\n<h2>What it is not</h2>\n<p>Incline walking is not a fat-loss hack (the energy cost scales with the work, like every exercise), not a replacement for strength training (the loads are bodyweight), and not risk-free on the downhill side &mdash; walking steep declines is harder on the knees than the ascent. Used as what it is &mdash; scalable, joint-friendly aerobic work with a strength flavour &mdash; it is one of the best return-per-effort hours available to a beginner. The treadmill is a tool; the grade dial is the part worth experimenting with.</p><h2>Progress without turning every walk into a test</h2><p>Start with a grade and duration you can repeat, then change one variable at a time. A small increase in incline can make the session substantially harder, so there is no need to raise speed and grade together. Keep posture tall, take shorter steps and avoid hanging your bodyweight from the rails; if you cannot walk comfortably without gripping, reduce the incline. The talk test is a practical intensity check: at a moderate effort you can speak in phrases, while a harder interval makes conversation difficult. Recovery between harder days is part of the training stimulus, not a missed opportunity.</p><h2>Make the setting match the goal</h2><p>For a steady aerobic session, choose a pace you can sustain and keep the grade consistent. For variety, alternate a few minutes at a steeper setting with easier walking rather than holding the maximum incline for the whole workout. If you have a history of balance problems, joint pain or a medical condition, get individual guidance before increasing the grade. Treadmill readouts are estimates, not measurements of calories or fitness; use repeatable time, incline and perceived effort to see whether the same session becomes easier over several weeks.</p><h2>Build a progression you can repeat</h2><p>Log the grade, pace, duration and how hard the session felt. If the same walk becomes easier over several weeks, that is progress; there is no need to chase a calorie estimate on the console. A short easy walk after a steep session can support consistency without adding another hard workout. Adjust for the treadmill’s calibration and stop if the belt, footwear or balance feels unsafe. The best setting is the one that lets you return next week feeling recovered enough to train again.</p>'),
('lactate-threshold-explained', 'Train', 'Lactate threshold, explained: the pace line that actually matters', 'What the threshold is, why &quot;lactic acid&quot; is the wrong phrase, and how the number changes what your training zones mean.', '<p>Every endurance conversation eventually reaches lactate threshold &mdash; the intensity at which hard work starts to feel qualitatively different. It is the most useful number in endurance physiology, and it is badly explained almost everywhere. Here is the honest version.</p>\n<h2>What is actually happening</h2>\n<p>Working muscles produce lactate continuously (it is a fuel, not a waste product &mdash; the &ldquo;lactic acid burn&rdquo; phrase is the wrong chemistry). At low intensities, production and clearance balance easily. As intensity rises, clearance keeps pace longer than people expect &mdash; until a turning point where the balance starts to slip and the effort must slow or stabilise. That turning point is the lactate threshold, and the full physiology is documented at <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lactate_threshold">Wikipedia&rsquo;s lactate threshold entry</a>. The practical experience matches the lab: below threshold you can hold a conversation; at and above it, the sentence breaks down.</p>\n<h2>Why the threshold is the useful number</h2>\n<p>Because it scales with the athlete. Two runners with identical 5k times can have very different easy paces but similar thresholds &mdash; which makes threshold the number that anchors training zones. Most of the training methods with the strongest evidence base are built around it: long work below, sustained work at, intervals above. The zone vocabulary used on this desk &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/zone-2-cardio-explained/">zone 2 cardio explained</a> &mdash; is essentially a map of where you sit relative to the threshold.</p>\n<h2>How it is measured (and estimated)</h2>\n<p>Gold-standard measurement is a lab step test with blood lactate sampling. Field estimates are nearly as useful for training: a 30-60 minute hard effort (like a well-paced half-marathon or a 30-minute time trial with the average of the last 20 minutes) lands close to threshold power or pace. Heart rate at threshold is a stable personal reference number. The honest accuracy note: field estimates are within a few percent for most people &mdash; good enough to set zones and much better than a formula from an age table.</p>\n<h2>What changes when you know yours</h2>\n<p>Three things. Easy days get genuinely easy (below threshold, not near it), which is what makes hard days possible. Interval targets become specific (4-8 minute efforts around or above threshold do the classic work). And progress becomes visible: threshold pace at the same effort is the cleanest adaptation signal in endurance training. For the muscular side of the same coin &mdash; how close to failure a set should end &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/rpe-and-rir-explained/">RPE and RIR explained</a>.</p><h2>Threshold is not one fixed biological switch</h2><p>Researchers and coaches use several related definitions, including the first and second lactate thresholds and the maximal lactate steady state. They do not always identify the exact same pace, and a field test will not reproduce a laboratory measurement perfectly. For day-to-day training, the distinction matters less than consistency: choose one method, record the conditions and compare like with like. Heat, hills, sleep, illness and accumulated fatigue can all shift the pace you can sustain, so a single disappointing session does not mean the underlying threshold has fallen.</p><h2>How to train around it sensibly</h2><p>Easy volume builds the base that lets an athlete tolerate harder work; threshold sessions add sustained practice near the effort that can be held for a prolonged period; faster intervals develop other qualities. More threshold work is not automatically better, and stacking hard days can reduce the quality of each one. A beginner can use breathing and conversational effort rather than testing a precise number. Anyone returning from injury or managing a health condition should progress gradually and seek qualified advice where appropriate. The metric is useful because it guides training, not because it needs to dominate it.</p><h2>Effort cues can be enough</h2><p>Not everyone needs a test or wearable to train well. A conversational easy run, a comfortably hard sustained effort and short intervals already give useful distinctions between intensities. If an athlete uses pace or heart rate, treat the target as a range and let terrain and conditions inform the day’s choice. The point of a threshold estimate is to organise training, not to turn every run into a test or to override signals from the body.</p>'),
('running-cadence-explained', 'Train', 'Running cadence, explained: steps per minute and the myths around it', 'What cadence measures, where the famous 180 came from, and why the number is a description — not a prescription.', '<p>Running cadence &mdash; steps per minute &mdash; is the metric most runners have heard of and few understand. The internet has turned it into a prescription (usually 180) that the physiology does not support. Here is what the number actually is.</p>\n<h2>What cadence measures</h2>\n<p>Cadence counts every step: both feet, per minute. Typical trained runners land around 160-180 at easy paces; elites run higher at race pace, and everyone&rsquo;s cadence rises naturally with speed. It is a description of how you solve the locomotion problem, not a performance target &mdash; the mechanics of the metric are in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadence_(running)">Wikipedia&rsquo;s cadence entry</a>. The single most useful training fact about it: cadence and stride length are the two inputs to speed, and cadence is the one that changes more gracefully with fatigue.</p>\n<h2>Where the 180 myth came from</h2>\n<p>The famous number traces to observations of elite runners at competition pace, later generalised into a rule. But it was never a law of physics: a tall runner at easy pace and a short runner at threshold pace have different optimal step rates, and forcing a number changes how you run rather than how well you run. The honest version: if your easy cadence is very low (well under 160) and you overstride (foot landing well ahead of your hips), nudging cadence up a few percent is a reasonable experiment &mdash; because it tends to pull the landing under the body. Beyond that, the number is biography, not doctrine.</p>\n<h2>What actually helps instead</h2>\n<p>Two adjustments do what people want cadence to do. Fix the overstride directly: think &ldquo;land under the hips&rdquo; or &ldquo;quick and light&rdquo; rather than counting steps. And let cadence rise naturally with speed work &mdash; strides, hill sprints and short intervals teach higher turnover far better than metronomes. If you do use a metronome, use it for short drills, not whole runs; running is a self-organising system and it finds its own economical solution when the training is sensible.</p>\n<h2>Reading your own numbers</h2>\n<p>Watches now log cadence for every run, and the useful habit is trend-watching: if cadence collapses late in long runs, that is a fatigue signal worth responding to with strength work and better pacing; if it is wildly inconsistent run to run, form is still negotiable. Compare yourself only to yourself. The metric earns its place as a mirror &mdash; the moment it becomes a target, it starts making runners worse.</p><h2>When a cadence change may help</h2><p>Cadence is worth investigating when it is connected to a real issue—overstriding, discomfort, difficulty holding form late in a run or a coach’s observation—not because an app flags a number below a target. If you experiment, increase turnover only slightly for short segments and let the body adapt over several sessions. A large jump can make running feel forced and move stress elsewhere. Strength, sensible pacing, adequate recovery and gradual mileage changes often matter more than the watch metric. If pain persists, stop treating cadence as a diagnosis and consult a qualified clinician.</p><h2>How to measure it consistently</h2><p>Most watches report steps per minute, but sensor placement and algorithms can create small differences between devices. For a simple check, count one foot’s contacts for 30 seconds and double that count to estimate total steps per minute. Compare at a similar pace and terrain; a slow recovery run should not be judged against a fast interval. The useful question is whether your cadence changes naturally as effort changes and whether your form remains comfortable—not whether you match an elite runner’s race-day figure.</p><h2>Cadence changes with the session</h2><p>Short hills, strides and faster intervals naturally increase turnover; recovery jogging lowers it. That variation is expected and is one reason a single average from a run can be misleading. Compare similar routes and paces, and do not use cadence alone to judge fatigue or injury risk. If a coach suggests a form change, practise it briefly and review how it feels over time. Comfortable, efficient running matters more than matching a universal number.</p>'),
('crossfit-explained', 'Train', 'CrossFit, explained: what the training system actually is', 'The methodology, the class structure and the Whiteboard — what CrossFit is, what it asks of you, and how it compares with fixed-format racing.', '<p>CrossFit is one of the most successful and most misunderstood training systems ever built. The pitch (&ldquo;constantly varied functional movements performed at high intensity&rdquo;) is accurate and almost useless to a beginner. Here is what that phrase means in a Tuesday evening class.</p>\n<h2>The methodology in practice</h2>\n<p>Every CrossFit workout is a short, prescribed session drawn from a common movement vocabulary: squatting, hinging, pressing, pulling, running, rowing, skipping, and the Olympic lifts and gymnastics skills that give the system its famous skill ceiling. The daily workout of the day (the WOD) changes constantly &mdash; the variety is the design, not chaos &mdash; and results are measured (time, rounds, load), which turns every session into a scored effort. The methodology&rsquo;s own definition and history are at <a href="https://www.crossfit.com/what-is-crossfit">crossfit.com</a> and in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CrossFit">Wikipedia&rsquo;s CrossFit entry</a>.</p>\n<h2>The class product</h2>\n<p>What you actually buy is a coached hour: warm-up, skill or strength piece, the WOD, cool-down. The coach scales the workout for every athlete in the room &mdash; the same session with lighter loads, fewer reps or simpler movements &mdash; which is the system&rsquo;s real innovation: one workout, thirty different athletes. The community structure (the Whiteboard, the shared suffering, the named hero workouts like <a href="https://www.crossfit.com/hero-workouts">Murph and its siblings</a>) is doing genuine behavioural work: attendance is the strongest predictor of results, and CrossFit is engineered for attendance.</p>\n<h2>What it demands &mdash; and where it asks more</h2>\n<p>Physically it is broad: strength, endurance, skill and mobility in rotation, so the system rewards generalists. The honest watch-outs are the two edges: the Olympic lifts and high-skill gymnastics have real learning curves (scaling is not optional, it is the method), and high-intensity work every session needs the dose managed &mdash; the beginner mistake is treating every WOD as a competition. The comparison with the fixed-format alternative is in <a href="/fitness/hyrox-vs-crossfit-explained/">Hyrox versus CrossFit</a>; the short version is that CrossFit is a training practice and Hyrox is a race.</p>\n<h2>How to start well</h2>\n<p>Three rules for the first month. Take the fundamentals class (every affiliate runs one) and treat it as the real start. Log your scaling choices rather than your scores &mdash; consistency of attendance beats intensity of effort early. And ask the coach about load before the workout, not after. Done that way, CrossFit is what its best practitioners say it is: a scalable, coached, measured general fitness practice with an unusually good attendance engine attached.</p><h2>Scaling is part of the programme</h2><p>A good coach can adjust load, range of motion, repetitions and movement choice while preserving the intended stimulus. If the workout calls for a fast, repeatable effort, scaling a complex lift to a simpler movement may be more faithful to the plan than attempting a load that breaks form. Beginners should expect to learn technique before chasing the clock, and experienced athletes still scale on days when fatigue or injury changes what is appropriate. A competitive score is not the measure of whether a session was worthwhile.</p><h2>Choosing a box and managing risk</h2><p>Visit a class, ask how coaches handle new members and watch whether movement standards are taught clearly. Ask about the introductory course, class size, equipment, cancellation terms and how the gym responds when someone reports pain. High-intensity training is not inherently unsafe, but fatigue and complex movements deserve sound coaching and sensible progressions. A well-run class welcomes questions and offers modifications without embarrassment. If a coach encourages you to ignore sharp pain or perform a movement you have not learned under load, that is a reason to pause and reassess.</p><h2>How to judge progress</h2><p>Keep a record of the workout, the load, the scaling and how the session felt. A faster score only means improvement if the movement standard and conditions are comparable. Repeating a benchmark months later can be informative, but chasing personal records every class invites poor pacing and rushed technique. Consistency, sound movement and the ability to recover for the next session are better early signs that the programme is working than one impressive time on the board.</p>'),
('gym-etiquette-explained', 'Train', 'Gym etiquette, explained: the unwritten rules, written down', 'Racking, repping and the shared-space arithmetic — the norms that make a busy gym work, stated once and plainly.', '<p>Gym etiquette is a set of norms nobody teaches and everybody is judged by. The rules vary by gym but the arithmetic never does: a shared machine is a queue, and the currency is time. Here is the unwritten part, written down.</p>\n<h2>The equipment rules</h2>\n<p>Three norms cover most of it. <b>Re-rack your weights</b> &mdash; every plate left on a bar is work someone else must do before they can train. <b>Wipe what you sweat on</b> &mdash; the towel-and-spray habit is the price of shared upholstery. <b>One machine at a time during busy hours</b> &mdash; supersets across three stations are a fine training method at 10am and a social offence at 6pm. The rest of the equipment code follows from these: return dumbbells to the rack rather than the floor, unload bars you loaded, and treat the clips as part of the equipment.</p>\n<h2>The space rules</h2>\n<p>The floor has grammar. Stand clear of the platform or rack someone is using &mdash; not near, clear. Walk behind lifts rather than in front of someone&rsquo;s line of sight to the mirror. Keep the bag and the bottle in the corner, not beside the bench you are resting on. And the mirror is the lifter&rsquo;s tool first: if someone is working sets, the mirror space in front of them is spoken for. In crowded gyms, the single most common friction &mdash; hovering during someone&rsquo;s rest &mdash; is solved by asking: &ldquo;Are you still working this?&rdquo; The answer is almost always yes, and the conversation takes four seconds.</p>\n<h2>The social rules</h2>\n<p>Headphones mean conversation-optional. Advice is welcome when asked for and noise when not &mdash; the one exception is genuine danger. Chalk stays over the platform. Grunting is fine; dropping a deadlift is gym-specific (ask or watch); filming is a permission question, always &mdash; other people are not content. And the universal norm under all of them: the beginner&rsquo;s presence is never the problem &mdash; the etiquette exists so that everyone, at every level, can use the hour they paid for.</p>\n<h2>Why the norms exist</h2>\n<p>Strip away the folklore and the norms are a coordination system for scarce equipment under time pressure &mdash; the same reason libraries have quiet rules. Gyms that post their own house rules have simply written down their local variant (equipment reservations, chalk bans, dress codes); the house rule wins over this page or any other. But in any gym on earth, the person who re-racks, wipes and asks before working in is the person everyone is happy to see at 6pm.</p><h2>Sharing equipment without losing your session</h2><p>When a gym is busy, ask whether someone is using a station before moving plates or towels. If the equipment is occupied, offer to work in only when the exercises and loading make that practical; not every bench or machine can be shared safely between sets. Keep rest periods reasonable without rushing a heavy lift, and return attachments to the place the gym marks for them. If a piece of equipment is broken, tell staff rather than leaving the next person to discover it mid-set. Small signals—asking, wiping, reracking—keep the floor predictable.</p><h2>Filming and personal space</h2><p>Recording a set can help with technique, but frame the camera tightly and check that other members are not identifiable in the background. Follow the facility’s filming policy and stop if someone asks not to appear. Do not place a phone where it blocks a walkway or creates a trip hazard. The same courtesy applies to unsolicited coaching: a quick offer to spot is helpful; a running commentary on another person’s form usually is not. Staff instructions and posted rules take precedence over informal gym customs.</p><h2>When something goes wrong</h2><p>If another member is using equipment unsafely or a disagreement develops, speak calmly and involve staff rather than escalating it on the training floor. Do not move a person’s belongings or assume a station is abandoned because they stepped away briefly. Staff can explain local rules on reserving racks, sharing benches and cleaning equipment. The simplest approach is to ask first, respect the answer and leave the station ready for the next person.</p>'),
('murph-workout-explained', 'Train', 'The Murph workout, explained: the hero WOD everyone quotes', 'What the workout actually is (and the partition rule), where it comes from, and how to approach it without becoming a statistic.', '<p>Every May, gyms fill with people in weighted vests doing the same workout: a mile run, 100 pull-ups, 200 push-ups, 300 squats, another mile run. It is called Murph, it is the most famous workout in functional fitness, and it is harder than the internet&rsquo;s photos suggest.</p>\n<h2>What the workout is</h2>\n<p>Murph is for time: <b>1 mile run, 100 pull-ups, 200 push-ups, 300 air squats, 1 mile run</b> &mdash; traditionally in a 20lb/14lb weighted vest. The standard rule allows the middle work to be partitioned however you like (the classic being 20 rounds of 5-10-15), and the runs bookend it. It is named for Lieutenant Michael Murphy, and it sits in the hero-workout canon published at <a href="https://www.crossfit.com/hero-workouts">crossfit.com/hero-workouts</a> &mdash; named workouts that memorialise fallen service members, a tradition that gives the suffering its context.</p>\n<h2>Why it is harder than it looks</h2>\n<p>The volume is the trap: 600 bodyweight reps is a muscular-endurance event disguised as a hero workout, and the second mile run arrives on legs that have done 300 squats. The vest multiplies the cost of every rep and every stride. The push-up count is what breaks people &mdash; 200 push-ups is a serious number for anyone whose chest work is accessory volume. None of it is technically hard; all of it is hard in aggregate.</p>\n<h2>How to approach it honestly</h2>\n<p>Four notes from people who have finished one. <b>Scale before you start</b>: the workout works at half volume, ring rows instead of pull-ups, or no vest &mdash; the day&rsquo;s stimulus survives scaling better than your shoulders do. <b>Partition deliberately</b>: breaking the middle work into small sets from the first round beats heroic early sets and a wall at rep 60. <b>Respect the day</b>: Murph is traditionally done on Memorial Day in the US &mdash; if that context matters to you, learn the story before the workout. And <b>recover after</b>: this is a high-damage session &mdash; the recovery guidance in <a href="/fitness/rest-days-and-recovery/">rest days and recovery</a> applies with interest.</p>\n<h2>Training for it</h2>\n<p>Eight weeks out, the useful preparation is unglamorous: weekly long runs for the bookends, high-rep bodyweight volume in small sets for the middle, and one practice session at reduced scale to learn your partition. The vest is the last thing added, not the first. And the honest measure of success is finishing well, not finishing fast &mdash; the workout&rsquo;s design already respects the person it is named for; the least you can do is survive it.</p><h2>Scale the movement, not just the number</h2><p>Pull-ups can be replaced with ring rows or band-assisted repetitions; push-ups can be elevated or performed in smaller sets; and the vest can be omitted. Those are not loopholes—they make the volume manageable while preserving the workout’s broad structure. A person who has not built up to high-repetition work should not use the event as their first exposure to hundreds of reps. Practise the movements over time, break sets early and stop if technique deteriorates or pain appears. Completing a scaled version with control is a better outcome than forcing the prescribed version once.</p><h2>Plan the day and the recovery</h2><p>Warm up with easy movement and rehearsal sets, choose a conservative pace for the first run and decide how you will partition the middle before starting. Keep water available and take breaks when needed; a timed workout is not a reason to ignore heat, dizziness or unusual shortness of breath. The next day, gentle movement and normal meals are usually more useful than trying to “make up” for soreness with another hard session. If you have a medical condition or have been inactive, discuss a demanding event with a clinician before taking part.</p><h2>Know when to stop</h2><p>Heat, dehydration, unusual chest symptoms, faintness or sharp pain are not ordinary workout discomfort. Stop, cool down and seek help when symptoms warrant it. Event organisers should provide a clear route, water and a way to contact staff; participants should know where those are before the clock starts. The workout is a voluntary challenge, not a test of whether someone can ignore warning signs. Finishing safely matters more than the vest, the partition scheme or the time written on a whiteboard.</p>'),
]

